    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-legislation-amendment-international-production-orders-act-2021-sch-1-sec-138-ss-2",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "138(2) Other records to be kept—Australian Designated Authority",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-legislation-amendment-international-production-orders-act-2021#sch-1-sec-138-ss-2"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-legislation-amendment-international-production-orders-act-2021.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-legislation-amendment-international-production-orders-act-2021.pdf, p.176",
      "values:originalText": "(2) If:\n\n(a) there is a designated international agreement between\nAustralia and one or more foreign countries; and\n\n(b) the agreement deals with (among other things) the issue of\norders (however described) by a competent authority\n(however described) of such a foreign country; and\n\n(c) such an order (the incoming order) is directed to a prescribed\ncommunications provider that:\n\n(i) carries on activities in Australia; or\n\n(ii) provides one or more services to end-users who are\nphysically present in Australia; and\n\n(d) the provider notifies an authority (however described) of the\nforeign country that the provider objects to the incoming\norder:\n\n(i) on the grounds that the incoming order does not comply\nwith the designated international agreement; or\n\n(ii) on similar grounds; and\n\n(e) the Australian Designated Authority is aware of the\nobjection;\nthe Australian Designated Authority must cause the following to be\nkept in the Australian Designated Authority’s records:\n\n(f) a record of the objection;\n\n(g) the name of the foreign entity that applied for the incoming\norder;\n\n(h) the name of the designated international agreement;\n\n(i) the name of the provider;\n\nSchedule 1  Amendments",
      "skos:definition": "(2) If:\n\n(a) there is a designated international agreement between\nAustralia and one or more foreign countries; and\n\n(b) the agreement deals with (among other things) the issue of\norders (however described) by a competent authority\n(however described) of such a foreign country; and\n\n(c) such an order (the incoming order) is directed to a prescribed\ncommunications provider that:\n\n(i) carries on activities in Australia; or\n\n(ii) provides one or more services to end-users who are\nphysically present in Australia; and\n\n(d) the provider notifies an authority (however described) of the\nforeign country that the provider objects to the incoming\norder:\n\n(i) on the grounds that the incoming order does not comply\nwith the designated international agreement; or\n\n(ii) on similar grounds; and\n\n(e) the Australian Designated Authority is aware of the\nobjection;\nthe Australian Designated Authority must cause the following to be\nkept in the Australian Designated Authority’s records:\n\n(f) a record of the objection;\n\n(g) the name of the foreign entity that applied for the incoming\norder;\n\n(h) the name of the designated international agreement;\n\n(i) the name of the provider;\n\nSchedule 1  Amendments",
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:telecommunications-legislation-amendment-international-production-orders-act-2021-sch-1-sec-138"
      },
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-legislation-amendment-international-production-orders-act-2021-sch-1-sec-138-ss-2-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-legislation-amendment-international-production-orders-act-2021-sch-1-sec-138-ss-2-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "808f291f103195bff55171d35efa6e9e14248fdab47ab80be5cd66f230d85148",
      "skos:note": "Keep records of objections to incoming orders from foreign countries.",
      "dc:description": "a record of the objection, the name of the foreign entity that applied for the incoming order, the name of the designated international agreement, and the name of the provider."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-legislation-amendment-international-production-orders-act-2021#sch-1-sec-138-ss-2",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "138(2) Other records to be kept—Australian Designated Authority",
      "values:kind": "subsection",
      "cof:pageNumber": 176,
      "values:originalText": "(2) If:\n\n(a) there is a designated international agreement between\nAustralia and one or more foreign countries; and\n\n(b) the agreement deals with (among other things) the issue of\norders (however described) by a competent authority\n(however described) of such a foreign country; and\n\n(c) such an order (the incoming order) is directed to a prescribed\ncommunications provider that:\n\n(i) carries on activities in Australia; or\n\n(ii) provides one or more services to end-users who are\nphysically present in Australia; and\n\n(d) the provider notifies an authority (however described) of the\nforeign country that the provider objects to the incoming\norder:\n\n(i) on the grounds that the incoming order does not comply\nwith the designated international agreement; or\n\n(ii) on similar grounds; and\n\n(e) the Australian Designated Authority is aware of the\nobjection;\nthe Australian Designated Authority must cause the following to be\nkept in the Australian Designated Authority’s records:\n\n(f) a record of the objection;\n\n(g) the name of the foreign entity that applied for the incoming\norder;\n\n(h) the name of the designated international agreement;\n\n(i) the name of the provider;\n\nSchedule 1  Amendments"
    },
